Undifferentiated Weight Loss
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Involuntary loss of >5% total body weight | Normal age-appropriate standard malignancy screening | Preserved appetite with negative malabsorption screen

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-047
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of extreme weakness leading to an inability to safely mobilize, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute severe metabolic derangement or evolving paraneoplastic collapse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult offspring
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate severe, acute-on-chronic generalized weakness and physical decline in the setting of rapid weight loss. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an acute Addisonian crisis, severe electrolyte imbalance, or profound anemia, preventing systemic collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I just have absolutely no energy left, I don't want to eat anything, and today my legs simply couldn't hold my weight anymore.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports progressive, unintentional loss of more than 5 percent of body weight over the last six months. There is an unexplained, profound decrease in appetite, accompanied by generalized weakness and loss of stamina. The patient notes loosening of clothing and a mild psychological apathy towards recovery. The patient denies any overt fever, night sweats, or chronic diarrhea. Tonight's severe decline features a complete inability to stand unassisted due to overwhelming exhaustion.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound due to profound subjective weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver exhaustion; high risk of malnutrition and pressure injuries.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/68 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically ill, apathetic, and cachectic but is in no acute respiratory distress. An incidental note from a recent presentation describes a persistent nausea sensation with an absence of true emesis and a normal abdominal examination, contributing to the poor oral intake but lacking an acute surgical cause.
Physical Exam: Physical inspection reveals noticeable mild muscle wasting in the temporal and thenar regions. Cardiopulmonary exam is clear. Abdomen is soft, scaphoid, and non-tender. Neurological exam demonstrates globally reduced muscle power (4/5 symmetric) consistent with generalized deconditioning, but lacks focal neurological deficits or signs of acute cord compression. Baseline vital signs remain normal and perfectly stable without orthostatic hypotension.
Environmental Observations: Untouched dinner tray sits on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular medications known to cause profound weight loss.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Weight Loss - Status: Severe exacerbation of frailty and deconditioning.
Differentials:

Adrenal Insufficiency (Addisonian Crisis) - Ruled out clinically due to stable hemodynamics, lack of profound hypotension, normal temperature, and absence of vomiting.

Severe Anemia - Ruled out for acute risk due to lack of resting tachycardia, normal SpO2, and non-pale conjunctivae.

Occult Malignancy - Heavily suspected as the underlying driver of the progressive cachexia, but ruled out as an acute, immediate life-threatening emergency tonight due to stable vitals and an absence of localized organ failure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s sudden collapse and failure to mobilize mandated an urgent after-hours evaluation to exclude an acute, reversible metabolic crisis or impending circulatory failure. Telephone triage accurately identified the high vulnerability of the cachectic patient. A comprehensive physical assessment confirmed absolute hemodynamic stability, the absence of an acute focal neurological event, and global sarcopenia rather than an acute adrenal or ischemic event. By differentiating this profound chronic decline from an immediate, reversible acute crisis, the clinician avoided a highly traumatic emergency department admission, securing the patient safely at home and shifting the focus to immediate nutritional rescue and urgent outpatient staging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-calorie, low-volume nutritional supplements. The expected investigation results include a normal comprehensive metabolic panel, complete blood count, and thyroid function tests lacking a clear driving etiology, which will mandate further advanced imaging via the regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To combat the severe muscle wasting, consume two tablespoons of calorie-dense nut butter or full-fat yogurt every time you take your regular medications; integrating high-yield calories into an existing, non-negotiable routine bypasses the severe lack of appetite and physically supports your muscle mass.
Home-Based Services: Urgent referral to community nursing for a comprehensive frailty assessment.
DME/Safety: Continue strict bed rest tonight and utilize a bedside commode.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for an extensive malignancy screen, advanced imaging (CT Chest/Abdo/Pelvis), and dietitian referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops severe abdominal pain, vomits blood, experiences a sudden loss of consciousness, or if their blood pressure drops rapidly resulting in a fainting episode.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; undifferentiated weight loss in this demographic carries a very high suspicion for occult malignancy requiring rapid, advanced imaging.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves continued functional decline and muscle wasting unless the underlying hypermetabolic or anorexic driver is formally diagnosed and treated.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The caregiver must seek immediate emergency medical attention if the patient becomes completely unrousable, or if they begin to vomit persistently.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are avoiding hospital admission tonight because your heart and blood pressure are stable; your weakness is due to severe, long-term calorie and muscle loss, which requires careful feeding, not emergency room interventions.
Actionable Steps: Provide very small, incredibly high-calorie snacks every two hours rather than attempting large meals, as the stomach has shrunken and cannot handle bulk volume.

